title: Why undo does not step HEAD back
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: rationale
summary: Rolling HEAD back one revision would unrecord what the watcher saw — facts still true on disk — so undo reverts in place once the watcher has written.

The obvious undo — move HEAD back one revision — is wrong as soon as the watcher runs.
Between the change you want back and the present sit the watcher's revisions: a file
touched, a directory renamed, a file gone. Rewinding HEAD over them ''unrecords facts
that are still true on disk'', and the metadata falls out of step with the filesystem the
daemon exists to track. The watcher's revisions are not yours to undo.

So the choice between rolling back and reverting is not yours either: it depends on what
has been written since, which is exactly what you are not watching. Undo makes it for
you — a rollback when your change is still the last thing in the log (cleanest: no
trace), a revert when it is not (the watcher's work stays). The same reasoning shapes
redo, which must survive the watcher writing between an undo and its redo: "move HEAD
forward one revision" is no more sufficient than its inverse was.

The origin a revision carries is what makes the distinction possible: the operation
types cannot say it, since a file arriving is a creation like any other
([[Revisions and operations]]).
